WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.002
Safe logging of file operations helps teams investigate behavior without
leaking the very data they are trying to protect.

2
00:00:07.002 --> 00:00:16.494
Useful events may include uploads, downloads, access denied decisions,
deletions, parser failures, scan results, retention actions, and
administrative changes.

3
00:00:16.494 --> 00:00:27.535
Logs should include correlation IDs and operation status where useful, but
they should avoid unnecessary file contents, secrets, tokens, regulated
data, and sensitive personal information.

4
00:00:27.535 --> 00:00:31.642
Audit events are especially useful for security-sensitive workflows.

5
00:00:31.642 --> 00:00:40.124
A file deletion, privileged download, failed authorization check, upload
rejection, or parser failure may be important during incident response.

6
00:00:40.124 --> 00:00:51.098
Logging should be privacy-aware and role-aware: enough detail to support
investigation and accountability, not so much detail that logs become a
second uncontrolled copy of sensitive files.

7
00:00:51.098 --> 00:00:54.195
Testing should include more than happy-path uploads.

8
00:00:54.195 --> 00:01:07.188
Boundary tests, invalid names, unexpected extensions, oversized files,
empty files, parser failures, unauthorized downloads, deleted files,
concurrent operations, cleanup behavior, and access control checks all
matter.

9
00:01:07.188 --> 00:01:16.950
Path handling tests should confirm that file operations stay inside
intended directories without using offensive payload examples in course
material or documentation.

10
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.306
Code review should ask direct questions.

11
00:01:19.306 --> 00:01:20.989
Where did this path come from?

12
00:01:20.989 --> 00:01:22.403
Who chose this filename?

13
00:01:22.403 --> 00:01:24.423
What base directory constrains it?

14
00:01:24.423 --> 00:01:26.712
What permissions does the process have?

15
00:01:26.712 --> 00:01:28.597
What parser handles the content?

16
00:01:28.597 --> 00:01:30.011
What happens on failure?

17
00:01:30.011 --> 00:01:34.319
Is the file served back to users, and if so, where is authorization
enforced?

18
00:01:34.319 --> 00:01:42.600
Static analysis, dependency review, and secure coding standards can help,
but file handling must remain testable, reviewable, and observable.
